    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.add_balanced_tree - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.add_balanced_tree.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_balanced_tree()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Add a balanced k-ary tree to the current graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_balanced_tree()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Add a balanced k-ary tree to the current graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.add_balanced_tree()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Add a balanced k-ary tree to the current graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nAdd a balanced k-ary tree to the current graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L8062-L8185){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.add_balanced_tree(\n    k,\n    h,\n    type=None,\n    label=True,\n    rel=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Balanced tree](../assets/graphs/add-balanced-tree.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Three binary trees side by side for k=2 and heights h=1, 2, and 3: a root with two children, then two more levels of doubling, ending with eight leaves. Every internal node has exactly two children and all leaves sit at the same depth.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nCreates a tree where each internal node has exactly `k` children, with height `h`. The total\nnumber of nodes will be equal to `(k^(h+1) - 1) / (k - 1)` (or `h + 1` when `k=1`), and as\nin any tree there is one edge fewer than there are nodes.\n\nNodes are created level by level (breadth-first): the root first, then its `k` children,\nthen their children, and so on. Every edge joins a parent to one of its children, and in\na directed graph it points from parent to child, so the root is the only node with no\nincoming edges. The tree is appended to the graph without touching or connecting to\nexisting nodes.\n\nBalanced trees are useful for:\n\n- modeling hierarchies such as org charts, file systems, or taxonomies\n- generating test inputs for traversal, shortest-path, and tree algorithms with a known\n  depth and branching structure\n- comparing network measures on a perfectly regular hierarchy\n\nThe number of nodes grows exponentially with `h=`, so even moderate settings (say `k=4`,\n`h=10`, about 1.4 million nodes) get large quickly.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[k]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Branching factor: the number of children of every non-leaf node. Must be at least\n    `1`; `k=1` produces a simple path of `h + 1` nodes.\n\n<code>[h]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Height of the tree, i.e. the number of levels below the root (the root has height\n    `0`). Must be non-negative; `h=0` adds only the root.\n\n<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),\n    no `type` attribute is set.\n\n<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string; IDs run\n    in breadth-first order, so the root is `\"1\"` in an empty graph. A label that is\n    already used raises `ValueError` before anything is added. If `False`, nodes are\n    created without labels.\n\n<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge, e.g. `\"reports_to\"`.\n    If `None` (default), no `rel` attribute is set.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `k=` is less than `1` or `h=` is negative.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~add_full_rary_tree`) \n\n:   Add a full r-ary tree.\n\n[](`~add_binomial_tree`) \n\n:   Add a binomial tree.\n\n[](`~layout_bfs`) \n\n:   Place the nodes in layers by distance from a start node.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nIn a balanced tree every node but the leaves has `k` children, and every leaf is `h` levels\nbelow the root. A binary tree (`k=2`) of height `3` has `1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15` nodes. The\nnodes are numbered level by level, and in a directed graph the edges point down the tree:\n\n::: {#fd0f8527 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ntree = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_balanced_tree(k=2, h=3)\n\ntree.count_nodes(), tree.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)[:4]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(15, ['1->2', '1->3', '2->4', '2->5'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nWith `k=1`, every node has one child, so the tree is a path of `h + 1` nodes:\n\n::: {#f6fdd58b .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nec.Graph().add_balanced_tree(k=1, h=3).get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n['1--2', '2--3', '3--4']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Wide or deep\n\nA binary tree of height `3` on the left and a tree with three children per node and height\n`2` on the right, each with its root, node `1`, in the middle. They have almost the same\nnumber of nodes, 15 and 13, but the wider tree is one level shallower:\n\n::: {layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n\n::: {#b122c8e3 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nec.Graph().add_balanced_tree(k=2, h=3).layout_kamada_kawai().plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", label_by=\"label\", width=320, height=320, legend=False\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode